  1048	**4.4** **RQ** 3 **: Why do developers use functional**
  1049	**constructs, and in which scenarios?**
  1050	
  1051	
  1052	Table 10 reports the results of the open coding for what concerns
  1053	the reasons for using lambdas, comprehensions, and MRF, as well as
  1054	their procedural alternatives. As it can be seen, the first five reasons
  1055	are shared across both functional constructs and their procedural
  1056	alternatives. _Size_ is specific for using functional constructs, while
  1057	_Lack of knowledge_, _Project constraints_, and _Simplify debugging_ are
  1058	specific for preferring their procedural alternatives.
  1059	In the following, we provide a discussion of the reasons, together
  1060	with examples of what is being highlighted by our participants.
  1061	**Coding time.** When implementing or enhancing features, developers tend to opt for the paradigm that ensures better productivity.
  1062	Specifically, for lambdas, respondents mention the saved time instead of defining a function. Of course, the latter is true in all cases
  1063	where the function does not require to be used more than once in
  1064	the code, _i.e.,_ P 65 stated _“it’s a waste of time to define a function if_
  1065	_all I’m ever going to do with it is pass it to another function once”_ .
  1066	As regards the usage of comprehensions, the save in coding time
  1067	seems, as reported by P 184, directly related to the total number
  1068	
  1069	
  1070	
  1071	of lines to be written _“to gather together a sub-list”_ . For MRF, developers motivate their use because as stated by P 161, using MRF,
  1072	it is possible to _“effortlessly reuse a function throughout different_
  1073	_sections of your code”_ . However, deciding whether to use the functional/procedural alternative is a trade-off between saved coding
  1074	time and code readability, _i.e., “They save time and don’t compromise_
  1075	_the readability as much as lambda”_ (P 59 ).
  1076	As the last column of Table 10 shows, coding time may also be a
  1077	reason for opting towards the procedural alternative. However, by
  1078	preferring the procedural alternative, reduced coding time may lead
  1079	to less readable code, hence they may be preferred when nobody
  1080	else has to look at the same piece of code, _i.e.,_ P 1 stated that _“ if_
  1081	_I am going to be the only looking at my code I care less about how_
  1082	_clean it looks. It takes less time to just manipulate variables directly_
  1083	_rather than sending them to a function and getting the output.”_
  1084	**Ease of use.** Coding time is not the only way to measure developers’ productivity. A different proxy can be related to how simple
  1085	it would be to deal with the construct, taking into account the task
  1086	at hand. Specifically, for lambdas, P 194 stated that _“they are much_
  1087	_easier to write and can usually fit on one line”_ . About comprehensions, participants highlight the simplicity in translating the logic of
  1088	the feature to be implemented, _i.e.,_ P 133 stated _“the way I think can_
  1089	_be easily translated into the code”_ . For MRF, developers recognize the
  1090	simplicity in properly applying the same operation _“to all members_
